{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The semantic categorization of support tickets by the kind of issue they concern (a taxonomy of issue types)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Provides a coarse-grained classification of each ticket into a small set of issue kinds (Bug, Feature Request, Billing, etc.), directly realising the query's request for the main kinds of issues."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_type",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Provides a finer-grained sub-classification of the issue, adding a second level of granularity to the same taxonomy focus rather than a distinct concept."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.95,
  "structure_quality": 0.85,
  "focus_actionability": 0.9,
  "rationale": "Both added columns centre tightly on ONE coherent, data-inferable focus: a taxonomy of the issue kinds represented in the tickets, which is exactly what the query asks for. They are not a scattered grab-bag nor mechanical features (no token counts or bare sentiment). The structure is sound: issue_category gives a coarse partition and issue_type gives a finer-grained partition, forming a two-level hierarchy at a useful granularity that is neither a single restatement nor undifferentiated noise. The minor weakness is that the two levels overlap somewhat (e.g., 'Authentication', 'Security', 'Integration' appear at both levels), so the coarse/fine distinction is not cleanly nested, slightly reducing structure_quality. The focus is highly actionable: an analyst can aggregate ticket volume by category, cross with priority/resolution_minutes, and prioritise support improvements."
}
